<section class="section-content bg-sec-blue" id="gst-health">
    <div class="il-container">
        <div class="mid-width">
            <h2 class="heading2 align-center">0% GST on Health Insurance<sup>AO</sup> </h2>
            <p class="text align-center mt-10">Yes, you read that correctly! The Goods and Services Tax (GST) on health insurance premiums has been reduced to 0% by the GST Council. This exemption means you pay 18% less while enjoying the same comprehensive cover. The change makes health insurance more affordable and accessible than ever for you and your family. </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section-content bg-pink read-more-list flex-wrap" id="terminology">
    <div class="il-container">
        <h2 class="heading2 align-center">Terminology in Health Insurance </h2>
        <div class="num-block">
            <ul>
                <li class="num-row">
                    <span class="num-txt">1</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Sum Insured: </strong> It is the maximum amount your insurer will pay in a policy year. Choose it wisely. If you choose a lower sum insured, you may end up paying the difference out of pocket during a major hospitalisation.</p>
                </li>
                <li class="num-row">
                    <span class="num-txt">2</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Deductible: </strong> This is the portion of your claim amount you agree to pay before the insurer steps in. Opting for a voluntary deductible can reduce your premium.</p>
                </li>
                <li class="num-row">
                    <span class="num-txt">3</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Co-payment: </strong> This is a cost-sharing arrangement where you cover a fixed percentage of the claim and the insurer covers the rest. Example: 10% co-pay means you pay ₹1,500 on a ₹15,000 hospital bill. </p>
                </li>
                <li class="num-row">
                    <span class="num-txt">4</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Waiting Period: </strong> This refers to the time you must wait before certain benefits kick in. Most policies have a 30-day initial waiting period for illnesses (accidents are usually covered from day one).</p>
                </li>
                <li class="num-row more-list-show">
                    <span class="num-txt">5</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Premium: </strong> This is the amount you pay to keep your policy active. Think of it like a subscription for your health coverage. </p>
                </li>
                <li class="num-row more-list-show">
                    <span class="num-txt">6</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Pre-existing Diseases: </strong> It refers to any medical conditions you had before your policy start date. Coverage for PEDs typically kicks in after a waiting period of 1–3 years.</p>
                </li>
                 <li class="num-row more-list-show">
                    <span class="num-txt">7</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Add-ons/Riders: </strong> These are optional extras that enhance your base coverage. Includes add-ons like OPD cover, maternity cover, worldwide cover, or critical illness riders. You pay a little more; you get a lot more.</p>
                </li>
                <li class="num-row more-list-show">
                    <span class="num-txt">8</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Reset BenefitS: </strong> Your sum insured gets restored even after it is exhausted, unlimited times. So even if you claim multiple times in a year, you never run out of cover.</p>
                </li>
                <li class="num-row more-list-show">
                    <span class="num-txt">9</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Exclusions: </strong> This is what your policy does not cover. Always read these carefully as knowing your exclusions upfront prevents unpleasant surprises at claim time.</p>
                </li>
                <li class="num-row more-list-show">
                    <span class="num-txt">10</span>
                    <p class="num-cont"><strong>Cashless Treatment: </strong> You walk in, get treated, walk out. The insurer settles the bill directly with the network hospital. No upfront payment needed from you.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <a href="javascript:;" class="read-more-link">Read More</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section-content owl-div" id="choose-health-policy">
    <div class="il-container">
        <h2 class="heading2 align-center">How to Choose the Best Health Insurance Policy? </h2>
            <p class="text sub-txt mt-30">Do not just buy the first health insurance plan you see. Follow this simple guide to choose the best health insurance for yourself and your family:</p>
        <div class="option-block owl-block owl-carousel owl-theme">
            <div class="option-item">
                <h3 class="heading3">Start with your actual needs:</h3>
                <p class="text">Are you buying for yourself, your family, or your parents? Do you have any pre-existing conditions? Do you travel frequently? Your answers should shape your coverage, not the other way around.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="option-item">
                <h3 class="heading3">Compare, do not just browse:</h3>
                <p class="text">Look at sum insured, waiting periods, network hospitals, add-on options, and claim settlement process side by side. A health insurance plan that looks cheap may cost more in the long run if it has high co-pays or sub-limits.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="option-item">
                <h3 class="heading3">Pick the right sum insured:</h3>
                <p class="text">Medical costs are rising. A ₹5 lakh cover that felt adequate five years ago may barely cover a week-long hospitalisation today. Factor in your city, your age, and your lifestyle.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="option-item">
                <h3 class="heading3">Read the inclusions and exclusions:</h3>
                <p class="text">This is the fine print that matters. Know what is covered and what is not, especially for chronic conditions, maternity, and specific treatments.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="option-item">
                <h3 class="heading3">Check the hospital network:</h3>
                <p class="text">The bigger the network of cashless hospitals near you, the easier it is to get treated without arranging upfront funds.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="option-item">
                <h3 class="heading3">Look at the insurer's track record:</h3>
                <p class="text">Claim settlement ratio and customer reviews are good indicators of whether your insurer will be helpful when you actually need them.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

